Infrastructure Migration Plan: K8s to Docker Compose
Project: PUCAR Infrastructure Transition
1. Executive Summary
This document outlines the strategic transition of the PUCAR ecosystem from an OpenShift (Kubernetes) environment to a Docker Compose architecture. The primary goal is to reduce operational complexity and resource overhead while maintaining service availability.
2. Architectural Comparison
The migration involves a shift from automated, distributed orchestration to a localized, service-defined model.
Feature
Kubernetes (OpenShift)
Docker Compose (Target)
Orchestration
Automated Pod Scheduling
Static Container Definition
Scaling
Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)
Manual (--scale command)
Service Discovery
Kube-DNS / CoreDNS
Docker Embedded DNS
Persistence
Persistent Volume Claims (PVC)
Named Volumes / Bind Mounts
Traffic Mgmt
Ingress / Routes
Nginx Reverse Proxy
Health Mgmt
Liveness & Readiness Probes
healthcheck & restart policies

3. Risk Assessment & Mitigation
High Alert: The move to Docker Compose removes the "High Availability" (HA) layer provided by K8s nodes.
Risk
Impact
Mitigation Strategy
Single Point of Failure
High
Enable restart: always. Implement external "Dead Man's Snitch" monitoring.
Storage Exhaustion
High
Set up a Cron job for docker system prune -f and monitor host disk space via Prometheus Node Exporter.
Manual Updates
Medium
Use a CI/CD runner (e.g., GitHub Runner) on the VM to automate docker-compose pull && docker-compose up -d.
Log Bloat
Medium
Configure logging driver limits in the compose file to prevent disk fill-up from JSON logs.
4. Functional Gaps (K8s Features Lost)
    • Self-Healing: Compose will restart a crashed container, but it cannot move it to a "healthy node" if the underlying hardware fails.
    • Zero-Downtime: Updates will involve a brief window of service unavailability during container recreation.
    • Resource Quotas: While cpus and memory limits can be set in Compose, they are not as strictly enforced or managed as K8s requests/limits.
5. Post-Migration Monitoring
To compensate for the loss of OpenShift's built-in monitoring:
    • Metrics: Deploy a node-exporter and cadvisor container.
    • Visualization: Centralize metrics in a lightweight Grafana instance.
    • Logging: Use the json-file driver with max-size: "10m" and max-file: "3".

References: 
    1. Docker Vs Kubernetes
    2. Pros and cons of containerization using docker
